# ============================================================================
# Embroidery Commerce Platform — protected environment variables
# Copy to `.env-ignore`. Contains variable NAMES only — never a value.
# ============================================================================
#
# Every name listed here marks a variable in `.env` whose *value* is a secret.
# The rule it activates is defined in `docs/09-SECURITY-AND-ABUSE-PREVENTION.md`
# §9a and summarised in `CLAUDE.md` §8a:
#
#   - the value must be requested from the human operator for the run that needs
#     it, never read out of `.env` automatically;
#   - it is never echoed, logged, cached, committed, written into a report or
#     fixture, or passed as a command-line argument;
#   - handing the whole file to tooling that consumes it itself
#     (`docker compose --env-file .env …`) stays allowed — the secret goes
#     file → process without being read out.
#
# The separate, absolute rule — never write to `.env` at all, for any variable —
# does not depend on this file.
#
# Format: one variable name per line. `#` starts a comment. Blank lines ignored.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This list is NOT the only protection. Two safety nets apply independently:
#
#   1. NAME PATTERN — a variable whose name contains PASSWORD, PASSWD, SECRET,
#      TOKEN, KEY, CREDENTIAL or PRIVATE is protected even when absent here, so
#      a newly added secret is covered before anyone edits this file.
#   2. MISSING FILE — if `.env-ignore` does not exist, every variable is treated
#      as protected. Absence must mean maximum caution, not open season.
#
# So this file exists to (a) name secrets the pattern cannot guess, and
# (b) let you widen protection deliberately. It can only ever ADD protection.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Operator account -------------------------------------------------------
STAFF_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD

# --- Database ---------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_PASSWORD
# Not secret-looking by name, but embeds POSTGRES_PASSWORD in the connection
# string — exactly the case the name pattern cannot catch.
DATABASE_URL

# --- SonarQube --------------------------------------------------------------
SONARQUBE_DB_PASSWORD
SONAR_TOKEN
